ormat explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\n2: 1, A: 2, Q: 1, J: 1, 9: 2, 7: 1\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♦8\nsingle: ♥Q\nsingle: ♥K\nsingle: ♠2\nsingle: BlackJoker\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**Decision Time: Playing My Hand**\n\nOkay, here\'s the situation. Landlord just played a 6. My teammate is next and has only two cards left, while the Landlord has six. I\'ve got a solid hand, three pairs of high cards (8s, Queens, Kings) and a single 2 and the Black Joker. My initial thought is to play the 2 - it\'s a high single and it can take the trick, which is important given the state of the game. I have to think about what to play - a single 2, or a pair or play the BJ? Playing the 2 preserves my hand structure. But I am getting ahead of myself, let\'s think.\n\nFirst, I need to understand what the other players might have. The "Unknown" list is the key - it tells me what cards are *not* in play, so I know the Landlord and my teammate have those cards. My calculations indicate that the Landlord and Teammate combined have precisely these missing cards: one 2, two Aces, one Queen, one Jack, two 9s, and one 7. Crucially, no Kings are left. This is critical.\n\nSo, back to the 2. The 2 is my best option. If I play it, and the Landlord has the other 2, I can play the Black Joker. I can also play the pair afterwards. My pair and single strategy is better and I want to force the game to be about pairs - that plays to my strengths. So 2 seems better than Black Joker as it preserves my hand. My teammate is holding two cards and is likely holding a pair, which means that passing is very dangerous here. The current turn is my best chance to win the lead, protect my teammate and maximize my chances of winning. So, I will play the ♠2.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has played a small single (♣6). My teammate (next player) has only 2 cards left. My hand is very structured with three pairs (88, QQ, KK) and two high control cards (2, BJ). Passing is risky because if my teammate holds a pair (a common holding when down to 2 cards), they cannot play on a single, allowing the Landlord to maintain control. Playing a \'middle\' card like a Q or K would break my pairs, leaving me with single \'junk\' cards and reducing my efficiency. Playing the ♠2 is the strongest move. It preserves my pair structure, almost certainly wins the lead (only the other 2 or my own BJ are higher), and allows me to switch the game to pairs by leading 88 next turn. This strategy gives my teammate a chance to win immediately if they hold a pair, or allows me to use my strong pairs to exhaust the Landlord.\n\nReason: My hand structure is best suited for playing pairs. To switch the lead to pairs, I need to win the current trick. Playing the ♠2 allows me to take control without breaking my pairs. This also protects my teammate, who might be stuck with a pair and unable to beat the single 6. If I win the lead, I can play my pair of 8s, potentially letting my teammate go out or forcing the Landlord to play high pairs.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠2"]}'}
